---
layout: article
title: Appwrite Push
description: Deliver push notifications to Android, iOS, web, Flutter, and React Native apps straight from Appwrite, without Firebase or device tokens.
back: /docs/
---
Appwrite Push is Appwrite's own push provider. With the Appwrite SDK, your app connects to Appwrite's push broker, an MQTT server that relays messages to connected devices. Messages you send with Appwrite Messaging arrive over that connection, so you can reach a signed-in user without setting up Firebase or collecting device tokens.

Appwrite Push works with the Web, Flutter, React Native, Android, and Apple SDKs. On Android, it keeps delivering after your users leave the app. On iOS, apps built with the Apple SDK use [APNs](/docs/products/messaging/apns) to show messages while the app is suspended.

# How it works {% #how-it-works %}

- **Every user has their own topic.** A topic is a named channel that devices subscribe to. A signed-in user can subscribe to `users/<USER_ID>` without creating a topic first. A message sent with `users: ['<USER_ID>']` reaches all of that user's connected devices.
- **You need targets only for some features.** A target is a record of one device that Appwrite delivers to. You don't need one to send to a user. Register an [Appwrite push target](#push-target) to use topics, iOS background delivery, or Appwrite Push alongside FCM or APNs.
- **The SDK holds the connection.** `Push` connects with the session the app signed in with, so you don't manage a separate token. When the user is blocked or deleted, Appwrite closes their connections. Signing out doesn't close an open connection, so call `push.close()` when the user signs out.
- **Missed messages are replayed.** With the default delivery guarantee, Appwrite keeps each message for up to 7 days and replays the ones a device missed when it reconnects.
- **Clients only receive.** Devices can't publish messages. Everything is sent from your server or the Console through [Messaging](/docs/products/messaging/messages).

{% section #add-provider step=1 title="Add provider" %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Add provider page, with Appwrite listed under Push](/images/docs/messaging/providers/appwrite/dark/provider-picker.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Add provider page, with Appwrite listed under Push](/images/docs/messaging/providers/appwrite/provider-picker.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Navigate to **Messaging** > **Providers** > {% icon icon="plus" size="m" /%} **Create provider** and choose **Appwrite** under **Push**.

{% only_dark %}
![The Configure Appwrite form with the provider name filled in](/images/docs/messaging/providers/appwrite/dark/provider-form.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Configure Appwrite form with the provider name filled in](/images/docs/messaging/providers/appwrite/provider-form.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Enter a name for the provider. Appwrite Push doesn't take any credentials. Optionally, change the two delivery settings below, then click **Create provider**:

- **Quality of service**: the delivery guarantee for devices that are connected when you send. **At most once (QoS 0)** sends each message once, and the device doesn't confirm it. **At least once (QoS 1)** waits for the device to confirm each message, so a message can arrive twice but isn't lost on the way. **Let the subscriber choose** sends at least once, and an app's subscription can switch to at most once with `retry: false`. Messages a device missed while offline are replayed according to the topic's and the subscription's setting. See [Offline replay](#offline-replay).
- **Message expiry (seconds)**: how long, in seconds, messages are kept for offline devices, up to 7 days (`604800`). Topics without their own expiry use the lowest expiry across your enabled Appwrite providers, or 7 days if none sets one. `0` keeps nothing for replay.

You can also create the provider with a [Server SDK](/docs/sdks#server).

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')                           // Your project ID
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');                                 // Your secret API key

const messaging = new sdk.Messaging(client);

const provider = await messaging.createAppwriteProvider({
    providerId: sdk.ID.unique(),
    name: 'Appwrite Push',
    qos: 1,          // optional
    expiry: 86400    // optional
});
```
```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.messaging import Messaging
from appwrite.id import ID

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') # Your API Endpoint
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')                           # Your project ID
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')                                  # Your secret API key

messaging = Messaging(client)

provider = messaging.create_appwrite_provider(
    provider_id = ID.unique(),
    name = 'Appwrite Push',
    qos = 1,         # optional
    expiry = 86400   # optional
)
```
```server-php
<?php

use Appwrite\Client;
use Appwrite\ID;
use Appwrite\Services\Messaging;

$client = (new Client())
    ->setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    ->setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')                           // Your project ID
    ->setKey('<API_KEY>');                                 // Your secret API key

$messaging = new Messaging($client);

$provider = $messaging->createAppwriteProvider(
    providerId: ID::unique(),
    name: 'Appwrite Push',
    qos: 1,         // optional
    expiry: 86400   // optional
);
```
{% /multicode %}
{% /section %}

{% section #install-sdk step=2 title="Install the SDK" %}

Appwrite Push is in the Web SDK 29.0.0, Flutter SDK 28.0.0, React Native SDK 2.0.0, Android SDK 30.0.0, and Apple SDK 21.0.0, and in later versions. Use Web SDK 29.2.0 and React Native SDK 3.1.0 or later, which keep reconnecting after the broker refuses a connection.

Install the SDK for your platform. Some platforms need extra setup:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #setup-flutter title="Flutter" %}
On Android, Push needs Android 7.0 (API 24) or later and core library desugaring. Add both to `android/app/build.gradle.kts`:

```kotlin
android {
    defaultConfig {
        minSdk = 24
    }
    compileOptions {
        isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled = true
    }
}

dependencies {
    coreLibraryDesugaring("com.android.tools:desugar_jdk_libs:2.1.4")
}
```

The `appwrite` 28.0.0 package needs Dart 3.10 and Flutter 3.38.1 or later.
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #setup-react-native title="React Native" %}
Install the SDK with its peer dependencies. Push needs native modules, so use a development or standalone build. It does not run in Expo Go.

```bash
npx expo install react-native-appwrite expo-file-system react-native-url-polyfill react-native-tcp-socket
# For background delivery and notification taps on iOS
npx expo install expo-notifications react-native-background-actions
```

Import the URL polyfill once, before the SDK:

```ts
import 'react-native-url-polyfill/auto';
```

On Expo SDK 52, set `config.resolver.unstable_enablePackageExports = true` in `metro.config.js`. The SDK doesn't use a config plugin. If you added `react-native-appwrite` to `plugins` in `app.json` while trying a release candidate, remove it and rebuild.
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #setup-android title="Android" %}
Add the SDK to your app module's `build.gradle.kts`:

```kotlin
dependencies {
    implementation("io.appwrite:sdk-for-android:30.0.0")
}
```

Push needs Android 7.0 (API 24) or later. The SDK's manifest declares the services, receivers, and permissions background delivery needs. The two permissions for punctual delivery are optional, and you declare them yourself. See [Delivery while the app is closed](#background-android).
{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #setup-apple title="Apple" %}
Add the package in Xcode under **File** > **Add Package Dependencies**, or in `Package.swift`:

```swift
.package(url: "https://github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-apple", from: "21.0.0")
```

Push needs iOS 15, macOS 12, tvOS 15, watchOS 8, or a later version. The `Appwrite` module has its own `App` type, so write `SwiftUI.App` for your app struct. For delivery while the app is suspended, also follow [Background delivery on iOS](#background-ios).
{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}
{% /section %}

{% section #subscribe step=3 title="Subscribe" %}

Sign the user in. Create a `Push` instance from the same client, then call `subscribe`. If you pass no topic, `subscribe` listens on the user's own topic, `users/<USER_ID>`. Messages you send to that user arrive on this topic.

{% multicode %}
```client-web
import { Account, Client, Push } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

await new Account(client).createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });

const push = new Push(client);

const subscription = await push.subscribe((message) => {
    const { notification, data } = JSON.parse(message.data);
    console.log(notification?.title, notification?.body, data);
});
```
```client-flutter
import 'dart:convert';
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';

final client = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

await Account(client).createEmailPasswordSession(email: email, password: password);

final push = Push(client);

final subscription = await push.subscribe(null, (message) {
  final body = jsonDecode(message.data) as Map<String, dynamic>;
  print('${body['notification']?['title']} ${body['data']}');
});
```
```client-react-native
import { Account, Client, Push } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

await new Account(client).createEmailPasswordSession({ email, password });

const push = new Push(client);

const subscription = await push.subscribe((message) => {
    const { notification, data } = JSON.parse(message.data);
    console.log(notification?.title, notification?.body, data);
});
```
```client-android-kotlin
import io.appwrite.Client
import io.appwrite.services.Account
import io.appwrite.services.Push

val client = Client(context)
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1")
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")

Account(client).createEmailPasswordSession(email, password)

// Pass an Activity so Push can ask for the notification permission
val push = Push(client, activity)

// subscribe can block, so call it off the main thread
val subscription = push.subscribe { message ->
    Log.d("push", message.data)
}
```
```client-apple
import Appwrite

let client = Client()
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1")
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")

_ = try await Account(client).createEmailPasswordSession(email: email, password: password)

let push = Push(client)

let subscription = try await push.subscribe { message in
    print(message.data)
}
```
{% /multicode %}

`subscribe` returns once the broker has accepted the subscription. On Android, a background subscription returns once the SDK's background service has taken it. Each message has `topic`, `data` (the payload as text), `payload` (the raw bytes), and `qos`.

`Push` connects with the session the app signed in with through this client, or a JWT or session you set on the client. Background delivery reconnects with the same credential, so prefer a session over a short-lived JWT.

On the web, the SDK can read the session only when your app and your Appwrite API are on different domains, such as your site and `<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io`. If they share a domain, for example through a custom API domain, set a JWT on the client before you subscribe: `client.setJWT((await account.createJWT()).jwt)`.

To follow the connection, register callbacks. Each holds one callback, and a later call replaces it.

```ts
push
    .onOpen(() => console.log('connected'))
    .onClose(() => console.log('disconnected'))
    .onError((error) => console.warn(error.message));
```

To change a subscription's options later, call `subscription.update(...)`. To stop it, call `subscription.unsubscribe()`.
{% /section %}

{% section #send-message step=4 title="Send a message" %}

Send a push notification to the user from the Console or a Server SDK, exactly as you would with FCM or APNs. Navigate to **Messaging** > **Messages** > {% icon icon="plus" size="m" /%} **Create message** > **Push**. Add a title and a body, and select the user as the recipient.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const message = await messaging.createPush({
    messageId: sdk.ID.unique(),
    title: 'Your order has shipped',
    body: 'Track it from the Orders tab.',
    users: ['<USER_ID>'],
    data: { orderId: '<ORDER_ID>' }
});
```
```server-python
message = messaging.create_push(
    message_id = ID.unique(),
    title = 'Your order has shipped',
    body = 'Track it from the Orders tab.',
    users = ['<USER_ID>'],
    data = { 'orderId': '<ORDER_ID>' }
)
```
```server-php
$message = $messaging->createPush(
    messageId: ID::unique(),
    title: 'Your order has shipped',
    body: 'Track it from the Orders tab.',
    users: ['<USER_ID>'],
    data: ['orderId' => '<ORDER_ID>']
);
```
{% /multicode %}

While the app is on screen, the message reaches your `subscribe` callback, and mobile apps show no system notification. Once the app is in the background, the SDK shows one. See [Notifications and taps](#notifications).

A message to a user needs no target or topic. Appwrite creates the user's topic the first time you send to it.

{% info title="Users with FCM or APNs targets" %}
Appwrite picks the delivery route per user, not per device. If any of the user's devices has an FCM or APNs target, Appwrite sends that user's messages only through FCM or APNs, and none of their devices receive them over Appwrite Push, web browsers included. The Apple SDK creates an APNs target when it subscribes with `background: true`. To keep Appwrite Push for all of the user's devices, register an [Appwrite push target](#push-target) on any one of them.
{% /info %}
{% /section %}

# Appwrite push target {% #push-target %}

A push target on the Appwrite provider tells Appwrite that a device listens over Appwrite Push. You need one to:

- Receive messages sent to [topics](#topics).
- Show messages while an iOS app is suspended. See [Background delivery on iOS](#background-ios).
- Receive messages over Appwrite Push when the user also has FCM or APNs targets. FCM still gets the full notification, so an Android app that registers both can show a message twice. Skip duplicates by `messageId`, or register only one of them.

Create the target after each sign-in. Appwrite Push doesn't use `identifier`, so pass any unique value. Always set `providerId` to your Appwrite provider's ID. Without `providerId`, Appwrite assigns the target to one of your enabled push providers, which may not be the Appwrite provider.

{% multicode %}
```client-web
import { ID } from 'appwrite';

const target = await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: ID.unique(),
    providerId: '<APPWRITE_PROVIDER_ID>'
});
```
```client-flutter
final target = await account.createPushTarget(
  targetId: ID.unique(),
  identifier: ID.unique(),
  providerId: '<APPWRITE_PROVIDER_ID>',
);
```
```client-react-native
import { ID } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const target = await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: ID.unique(),
    providerId: '<APPWRITE_PROVIDER_ID>'
});
```
```client-android-kotlin
val target = account.createPushTarget(
    targetId = ID.unique(),
    identifier = ID.unique(),
    providerId = "<APPWRITE_PROVIDER_ID>",
)
```
```client-apple
let target = try await account.createPushTarget(
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: ID.unique(),
    providerId: "<APPWRITE_PROVIDER_ID>"
)
```
{% /multicode %}

Targets belong to the session that created them. When the user signs out with `deleteSession`, Appwrite deletes that session's targets for you.

# Message format {% #message-format %}

Your callback receives the message as JSON in `message.data`. It contains `messageId`, `priority`, which defaults to `high`, and the fields you set when you sent it:

```json
{
    "messageId": "6720c5e7...",
    "notification": {
        "title": "Your order has shipped",
        "body": "Track it from the Orders tab.",
        "image": "https://example.com/parcel.png"
    },
    "data": {
        "orderId": "<ORDER_ID>"
    },
    "priority": "high"
}
```

`notification` can also carry `icon`, `color`, `sound`, `tag`, `channelId`, `badge`, `action`, `contentAvailable`, and `critical`, mirroring the fields of [`createPush`](/docs/references/cloud/server-nodejs/messaging#createPush). `createPush` takes `image` as a Storage file, `<BUCKET_ID>:<FILE_ID>`, and the message carries a URL to that file. With the default delivery guarantee a message can arrive twice, for example when it is replayed after a reconnect, so use `messageId` to skip duplicates.

# Topics {% #topics %}

To send the same message to many users, subscribe their devices to a [Messaging topic](/docs/products/messaging/topics) by its ID.

Topic messages are delivered through [Appwrite push targets](#push-target). Add the device's Appwrite push target as a [subscriber of the topic](/docs/products/messaging/topics#subscribe-targets-to-topics), from your server or from the app when the topic's permissions allow it:

```ts
await messaging.createSubscriber({
    topicId: 'news',
    subscriberId: ID.unique(),
    targetId: target.$id
});
```

Then subscribe to the topic in the app:

```ts
const news = await push.subscribe(['news'], (message) => {
    showNews(JSON.parse(message.data));
}, {
    background: true, // also notify when the app isn't on screen
    title: 'News'     // notification title when a message has none
});
```

Topic IDs must match exactly. Wildcards like `+` and `#` are refused, and of the `users/` topics, a user can subscribe only to their own. When the user is no longer a subscriber of the topic through any of their targets, Appwrite drops the device's live subscription to it.

Topics take quality of service and message expiry settings too, as the `qos` and `expiry` parameters of `createTopic` and `updateTopic`. A message is delivered at the lowest quality of service set by the provider, the topic, and the subscription. A topic without an expiry uses the lowest expiry of your enabled Appwrite providers, or 7 days if none sets one. See [delivery settings](/docs/products/messaging/topics#appwrite-push).

# Subscription options {% #subscription-options %}

{% table %}
* Option
* Description
* Default
---
* `background`
* Show a notification for each message while the app isn't on screen, and keep delivering in the background where the platform allows.
* `true` when you call `subscribe` without a topic, `false` when you pass topics
---
* `title`
* Notification title to use when a message has none.
* The topic name
---
* `retry`
* Ask for at-least-once delivery (QoS 1), so messages missed while offline are replayed.
* `true`
---
* `notifyInForeground`
* Also show a notification while the app is on screen. Android, Flutter, and React Native only.
* `false`
{% /table %}

# Notifications and taps {% #notifications %}

A subscription with `background` posts a notification for each message while the app isn't on screen, using the `title`, `body`, and `image` you sent. A message with only `data` posts no notification and only reaches your callback.

- **Android 13 and later**: the first background subscription asks for the notification permission. If the user declines, messages still reach your callback.
- **Android icon**: add a `meta-data` entry to your `<application>` with your icon. The key is `io.appwrite.push.notification_icon` for the Android SDK, `io.appwrite.flutter.push.notification_icon` for Flutter, and `io.appwrite.reactnative.push.notification_icon` for React Native.
- **Web**: the Web SDK shows a notification for each message on a background subscription while the tab is open, also while the tab is visible. Subscribe from a click handler, because some browsers only show the notification permission prompt after a user action. Mobile browsers don't show these notifications.

To open the right screen when the user taps a notification, read the tap that launched the app at startup and listen for later taps. `data` is the `data` you sent with the message.

```ts
// The tap that launched the app, reported once
const opened = await push.getInitialNotification();
if (opened) openOrder(opened.data.orderId);

// Taps while the app is running
const stop = push.onNotificationOpened(({ topic, data }) => openOrder(data.orderId));
```

On the web, `getInitialNotification()` always returns `null`, because a page only receives taps while it is open.

# Delivery while the app is closed {% #background %}

How long a device keeps receiving messages after the user leaves your app depends on the platform.

## Android {% #background-android %}

On Android, Flutter, and React Native apps, the SDK saves background subscriptions and wakes the app on a schedule to reconnect. The broker replays what was sent in between, and a notification is posted.

{% table %}
* Device state
* How often the app reconnects
---
* Android 11 and older, or exact alarms allowed
* About every 60 seconds, on time
---
* Exempt from battery optimization, no exact alarms
* About every 60 seconds, but Android can defer it
---
* Android 12 and later, neither of the above
* About every 15 seconds, but Android can defer it, for example in battery saver
---
* Doze (screen off, idle)
* About every 9 minutes
---
* Foreground service on
* Immediately, also in Doze
---
* Force-stopped
* When the app is opened again
{% /table %}

A message arrives at the next reconnect, so a deferred reconnect delays it.

For punctual wake-ups, declare these permissions in your manifest. On Android 14 and later, your users allow exact alarms under **Settings** > **Apps** > **Special app access** > **Alarms & reminders**. Google Play asks you to declare `SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM`.

```xml
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />
```

Background delivery is on for every subscription with `background` set, with nothing else to configure. `Push.backgroundStatus()` reports how punctual it can be. Its `bestEffort` flag is read-only and is `true` when messages can arrive late, or only once the app is opened:

- Exact alarms aren't allowed, and no foreground service is running. A foreground service doesn't count while background usage is restricted.
- The user restricted the app's background usage, and the app isn't exempt from battery optimization.

To make delivery more punctual, tell your users why you need the permission, then request it when they tap a button:

```kotlin
val status = Push.backgroundStatus(context)
if (status.bestEffort) {
    if (!status.exactAlarms) {
        Push.requestExactAlarms(context)
    }
    if (status.backgroundRestricted && !status.ignoringBatteryOptimizations) {
        Push.requestIgnoreBatteryOptimizations(context)
    }
}
```

`requestExactAlarms` and `requestIgnoreBatteryOptimizations` return `false` when the permission is already granted or not needed, or when the matching permission isn't declared in your manifest. Flutter and React Native have the same methods on `push`, without the `context` argument, and they're async there. Outside Android, `backgroundStatus()` returns `null`, and both request methods resolve to `false`.

For immediate delivery, `push.setForeground(true)` runs a foreground service with a quiet ongoing notification. Call it while the app is on screen. The setting is saved across restarts. It uses a `remoteMessaging` foreground service, which Google Play asks you to declare. If you never use it, remove the push service (`io.appwrite.services.PushService` on Android, `io.appwrite.flutter.services.PushService` on Flutter, `io.appwrite.reactnative.services.PushService` on React Native) and the `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_REMOTE_MESSAGING` permission from your merged manifest with `tools:node="remove"`.

## iOS {% #background-ios %}

These steps apply to apps built with the Apple SDK. Flutter and React Native apps on iOS receive messages only while the app runs and for a short time after it moves to the background. Once iOS suspends the app, messages wait until it opens again, and the broker then replays what was missed.

iOS suspends a backgrounded app within seconds, so the connection can't stay open. With the Apple SDK, Appwrite Push uses APNs to reach a suspended app:

1. When you send to a user who has an Appwrite push target, Appwrite sends their iOS devices a short APNs wake-up instead of the full notification.
1. A Notification Service Extension in your app opens its own connection, fetches the real message, and replaces the wake-up with its title, body, and image before iOS shows it.

To set it up:

1. In Xcode, add the **Push Notifications** capability and the **Background Modes** capability with **Remote notifications** to your app target.
1. Add an [APNs provider](/docs/products/messaging/apns). The quickest way is [one-click APNs setup](/docs/products/messaging/apns#one-click-setup): run `appwrite init apns` in your iOS project. The CLI creates the sandbox and production APNs providers and adds the Apple platform.
1. Add a **Notification Service Extension** target to your app, with this class:

    ```swift
    import Appwrite

    class NotificationService: AppwriteNotificationService {}
    ```

1. Give the app and the extension the **App Groups** capability with the same group ID, and set that group ID as `AppwritePushAppGroup` in the `Info.plist` of both targets. The extension reads the app's session from this group.
1. Set a client ID that you save per install with `client.setPushClientId(...)`. The default ID is shared by all of the user's iOS devices, so without your own ID, one device's extension can fetch the messages another device was woken for.
1. Register an [Appwrite push target](#push-target) after sign-in. Without it, Appwrite sends the full APNs notification instead of the wake-up.
1. Subscribe with `background: true`. The SDK registers the device with APNs and creates its APNs push target for you.

The SDK registers for remote notifications on its own. To handle registration yourself, set `AppwritePushAutoRegistration` to `NO` in `Info.plist`, call `UIApplication.shared.registerForRemoteNotifications()`, and pass the device token to `Push.setDeviceToken(_:)` from your app delegate. `Push.handleRemoteNotification(_:)` and `Push.isDelivered(_:)` are optional helpers.

## Web {% #background-web %}

Messages arrive while the page is open. To replay messages sent while the tab was closed, give each browser a stable client ID. See [Offline replay](#offline-replay).

# Offline replay {% #offline-replay %}

Appwrite stores each message until it expires. When a device subscribes again, Appwrite replays up to the 5 newest messages it missed on each topic, oldest first. Replay needs at-least-once delivery on both the topic and the subscription.

Appwrite tracks replay per client ID, the identifier a device connects to the broker with. A client ID the broker hasn't seen before gets no backlog.

- **Android**: the SDK derives a stable ID per user and install, so leave `setPushClientId` unset. This includes Flutter and React Native apps on Android.
- **iOS and the web**: all of a user's iOS devices and Flutter web sessions share the default ID. With the Web SDK, the default ID belongs to one browser tab. To replay missed messages, save a client ID per device, for example in the Keychain or `localStorage`, and set it with `setPushClientId`.

```ts
let clientId = localStorage.getItem('pushClientId');
if (!clientId) {
    clientId = crypto.randomUUID();
    localStorage.setItem('pushClientId', clientId);
}
client.setPushClientId(clientId);
```

All tabs of the browser share this ID, so a missed message is replayed to the first tab that reconnects.

A random client ID on every launch loses the backlog.

# Sign out {% #sign-out %}

Close `Push` when the user signs out. It closes the connection and stops background delivery, saved subscriptions included. Deleting the session doesn't close a connection that is already open.

```ts
push.close();
await account.deleteSession({ sessionId: 'current' });
```

# Self-hosting {% #self-hosting %}

A self-hosted Appwrite runs the broker in the `appwrite-mqtt` container. Traefik serves MQTT over TLS on port `8883` and MQTT over secure WebSocket on port `8084`. See the [MQTT environment variables](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/configuration/environment-variables#mqtt) to change the ports, the replay depth, and the maximum packet size.

By default, the Android, Apple, React Native, and Flutter mobile SDKs connect to `push.<YOUR_API_HOST>` on port `8883`, so a `push.` DNS record that points at your server is enough for them. The Web SDK and Flutter on the web connect to `wss://push.<YOUR_API_HOST>` on port `443`, which Traefik doesn't serve, so set the broker address on web clients. You can also set it on any client instead of adding the DNS record:

```ts
client.setPushEndpoint('mqtts://appwrite.example.com:8883'); // Android, Apple, React Native, Flutter on mobile
client.setPushEndpoint('wss://appwrite.example.com:8084');   // Web, Flutter on the web
```

A Flutter app that runs on both picks the endpoint with `kIsWeb`.

# Limits {% #limits %}

{% table %}
* Limit
* Value
---
* Message retention
* Up to 7 days
---
* Replay per topic
* Newest 5 missed messages
---
* Title and body
* 256 characters for the title and 64,230 for the body, the limits of `createPush`
---
* Connections
* 128 new connections per user per minute. On self-hosted Appwrite, this applies when `_APP_OPTIONS_ABUSE` is enabled.
{% /table %}

# Troubleshooting {% #troubleshooting %}

{% table %}
* Symptom
* Cause and fix
---
* `subscribe()` throws "needs a signed-in user"
* The user isn't signed in through this client, and no JWT or session is set on it.
---
* A topic subscription is refused
* The user isn't a subscriber of the topic, or the topic ID has a wildcard.
---
* Topic messages never arrive
* The device has no Appwrite push target subscribed to the topic.
---
* A user with FCM or APNs targets gets nothing over Appwrite Push
* Register an Appwrite push target for the device.
---
* Messages sent while offline never arrive
* A client ID shared by several devices, which is the default on iOS, or tied to one tab, which is the default with the Web SDK. Also check that `retry` isn't `false` and that the message hasn't expired. See [Offline replay](#offline-replay).
---
* No notification while the app is open
* Expected. Set `notifyInForeground` to `true` on Android, Flutter, or React Native.
---
* Nothing arrives while the Android app is closed
* Notification permission declined, the app was force-stopped, or exact alarms aren't allowed. Check `backgroundStatus().bestEffort`.
---
* iOS devices get no APNs notifications, or debug builds use the production provider
* No enabled APNs provider matches the app's bundle ID and build: sandbox for debug and Simulator builds, production for TestFlight and App Store builds. Rerun `appwrite init apns` to create both, or set the bundle ID on the provider in the Console. Devices pick up the change the next time the app launches.
---
* iOS shows "New message" instead of the real message
* The Notification Service Extension is missing, the extension target doesn't include the `Appwrite` library, or `AppwritePushAppGroup` isn't set to a group both targets share in both `Info.plist` files.
---
* The UI freezes or the app stops responding on Android
* Call `subscribe` off the main thread.
---
* React Native fails with "background delivery needs the SDK's native Android module"
* The app runs in Expo Go, or wasn't rebuilt after installing.
{% /table %}
